{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The relevant information for this reason includes the outcomes and the probabilities of obtaining a favorable outcome (i.e., a non-zero payoff) in each lottery. Lottery A offers a 33% chance of winning 2500 Euros and a 67% chance of 0 Euros, so the probability of a favorable outcome is 33%. Lottery B offers a 34% chance of winning 2400 Euros and a 66% chance of 0 Euros, so the probability of a favorable outcome is 34%. The outcomes differ (2500 vs 2400 Euros), but the decision reason explicitly states that outcome differences are considered negligible. Thus, the key comparison is the probability of a favorable outcome, which is slightly higher for Lottery B (34%) than for Lottery A (33%). According to the decision reason, Lottery B should be preferred because it has a higher probability of a favorable outcome, assuming the outcome difference is negligible.",
